#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087595 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bf8bb23-741b-5583-b51c-67eabae0ab54" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "07394b09-ab80-579c-9b10-dc7a2835d3c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-6595" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 25y =0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x}  = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2}  = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+25{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+25) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny = P\\cos{5x} + Q\\sin{5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087596 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "092a6c8c-7bea-5c53-88ac-f5dc8b16c357" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of a Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "b22fa1be-4c82-5dbf-b5fe-5cbb42c0c646" :question/math-academy-id "e-6596" :question/problem "Given that $y = y(t)$ satisfies the equation $y'' - 4y' + 85y = 0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?\n\n- $-2e^{2t}\\cos{9t} + 7e^{2t}\\sin{9t}$\n\n- $e^{2t}\\cos{9t}+3e^{2t}\\sin{3t}$\n\n- $-6e^{2t}\\sin{9t}$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y = e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda e^{\\lambda t}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+85{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +85) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 85 = 0,\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +85 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+85 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+81 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-81 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 9\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 9\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\lambda = 2 \\pm 9 \\textrm{i}$ are the complex roots of the characteristic equation, the general solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny = e^{2t} \\left( P \\cos 9t + Q \\sin 9t \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $P=-2$ and $Q=7.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II does *not* take the above form. It contains $\\sin 3t,$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III takes the above form with $P=0$ and $Q=-6.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087597 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "191e98e0-aa31-5dd9-a735-6580d29ad287" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complex Solutions of a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "7aa1ff4a-2263-5c99-97dd-d6fcada8bfc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6597" :question/problem "Consider the equation $y'' + 10y' + 34y  = 0,$ where $y = y(t).$ If $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then what is $\\lambda?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda e^{\\lambda t}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+10\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+34{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +34) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + 10\\lambda + 34  = 0,\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +34 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25)-25+34 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +5 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-5\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda = - 5 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087598 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2999a565-bf64-5246-9fee-54cecc9e79ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "04253a00-f441-58d8-b97e-f7935ae5c7c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-6598" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 4 \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 20y =0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x}  = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2}  = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our ODE gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+20{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +20) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +20 & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "092a6c8c-7bea-5c53-88ac-f5dc8b16c357" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of a Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "254da4f4-ae45-53ed-b8f1-38cf223decdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263323} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6267f4-54a6-5276-a597-89e17dce55ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263679} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e91013-3158-50a6-95bb-ceafcade3e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264367} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f46921-af99-5d26-a57f-b5feb00ce72c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x-2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264712} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f7f770-16a0-56b2-bff6-f2f328e949d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0e91013-3158-50a6-95bb-ceafcade3e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264367} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1fe5c38-0c8a-5ad6-a3d7-d8a42883f274" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "950cca0c-2751-5927-8f98-bdff08102121" :question/math-academy-id "q-130330" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+50y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+50{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +50) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +50=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +50 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+50 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+49 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-49 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 7\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 7\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =-1\\pm 7\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThus, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{(-1+7\\text{i})x})={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{(-1+7\\text{i})x})={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $50{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "092a6c8c-7bea-5c53-88ac-f5dc8b16c357" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of a Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ce005a0-0859-5d70-9762-50d37f1b33aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355051} {:answer/id #uuid "2fcbd114-4ac6-5a18-8de9-c463c3bb2781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355079} {:answer/id #uuid "505b0d61-8beb-5cf0-a448-20ac5c5e81c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355382} {:answer/id #uuid "5204b3bd-ccab-57fb-869b-6d69b871aa1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355398} {:answer/id #uuid "f514bbd4-5840-5cd4-a6bd-7f1ccbd48e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "505b0d61-8beb-5cf0-a448-20ac5c5e81c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355382} :answer-field/id #uuid "5910f9bd-500b-549c-bae8-5af3eb830f1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d14f94c-eee0-5194-aa26-bfd57687e00b" :question/math-academy-id "q-90807" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}-12{y}^{′}+40y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+40{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36)-36+40 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -6 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =6\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =6\\pm 2\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThus, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{(6+2\\text{i})x})={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{(6+2\\text{i})x})={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is not a solution."}]}
